    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Founded in 1501 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, the Safavid Empire reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> for the first time since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Mongol Ilkhanate</a>.</li><li>By imposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shia Islam</a> it gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> its lasting religious identity and turned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-shia-divide">Sunni-Shia divide</a> into a frontier with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</li><li>Weak harem-raised shahs and religious intolerance left it open to an Afghan revolt; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> fell in 1722 and the dynasty ended in 1736.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>After surrendering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1722, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-husayn">Shah Sultan Husayn</a> personally placed the crown on the head of the Afghan conqueror <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mir Mahmud Hotak</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

