    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-i.jpg" alt="Ismail I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shah_Ismail_I_Safavid,_Behzad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Safavid Empire was founded in 1501 when the fourteen-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safaviyya">Safaviyya Sufi order</a> based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>, a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes (traditionally seven) named for their distinctive red headgear, to victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sharur">Battle of Sharur</a>, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and proclaimed himself Shah. The Safaviyya order had been accumulating religious prestige and a military following across north-western Iran and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> for some two centuries, tracing its spiritual lineage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-al-din-ardabili">Safi al-Din Ardabili</a> (1252–1334), from whom the dynasty took its name. Ismail's ideological innovation, and the act that most decisively shaped subsequent Iranian and Middle Eastern history, was his immediate proclamation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shia Islam</a> as the state religion of Iran, a radical imposition in a region that was predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and had little recent tradition of Shia rule at the state level. The declaration served at once as a genuine religious commitment, a means of constructing an Iranian political identity distinct from the Sunni Ottoman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> empires on either side, and an instrument for mobilising the intense devotion of Qizilbash warriors who believed Ismail to be a manifestation of the divine. Within a decade of taking Tabriz, Ismail had conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> (including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1508) and parts of eastern Anatolia, unifying a territory that had been politically fragmented since the break-up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Mongol Ilkhanate</a> in the fourteenth century. The enabling conditions for this remarkable consolidation were the weakness of the Aq Qoyunlu after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>'s death in 1478, the cohesion of the Qizilbash tribal confederacy, and the messianic authority that Ismail projected with extraordinary personal charisma.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/new-julfa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-julfa.jpg" alt="New Julfa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/new-julfa">New Julfa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vank_Cathedral_courtyard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Safavid economy rested on three main pillars: taxation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>'s farming communities, long-distance trade along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> networks, and the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> monopoly that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> established in 1619 as a cornerstone of state revenue. Iranian agriculture depended on the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qanat">qanat</a></em> system of underground channels carrying water from mountain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifers</a> to lowland fields, which made cultivation possible across an otherwise arid plateau and supported a largely sedentary peasantry growing wheat, barley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> in the Caspian lowlands, and cash crops including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and, most importantly for Safavid commercial strategy, silk in the Caspian provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a>. Abbas I's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-julfa">New Julfa</a>, a suburb of his new capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, which uprooted the merchant community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julfa-nakhchivan">Julfa</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Aras</a> river in 1604–1605, was a deliberate economic policy: the Armenians possessed commercial networks reaching European markets and the linguistic and financial expertise to conduct the silk trade on terms favourable to the crown, including routes that bypassed Ottoman territory via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. The silk monopoly channelled revenue directly to the treasury, funding Abbas I's military reforms, building programme and diplomacy. Isfahan under Abbas I became one of the wealthiest cities in the world, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> linking Indian Ocean imports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, textiles and precious stones with European demand for luxury goods. The social hierarchy placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> military aristocracy at the apex in the early period, progressively displaced by Persian bureaucratic families and by the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a></em> slave-soldiers through Abbas I's deliberate institutional restructuring.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran.jpg" alt="Battle of Chaldiran" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_painting_in_Chehel_Sotoun1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amir Pashaei, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Safavid military underwent a fundamental transformation between the empire's founding and the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>, driven by the hard lesson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a> in 1514. There <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> deployed artillery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a>-armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissary</a> infantry against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> cavalry, whose leaders, by tradition, scorned firearms as unmanly; Ottoman firepower routed them in a single day, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> was briefly occupied, and the Safavids permanently lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakir</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, while the myth of Ismail's invincibility, the psychological foundation of Qizilbash commitment, was shattered. Ismail is said never to have led troops in battle again, and for nearly eight decades the army remained dependent on Qizilbash tribal cavalry that was both inadequate against Ottoman firepower and politically dangerous because of its commanders' independent power bases. Shah Abbas I's reforms from the 1590s onwards, in which the English adventurers Robert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-sherley">Anthony Sherley</a> are traditionally credited with a role (probably exaggerated), created new corps of <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a></em> cavalry, musketeers (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tofangchi">tofangchi</a></em>) and artillerymen (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topchi">topchi</a></em>), paid directly from the royal treasury and loyal to the Shah rather than to tribal chiefs. This reformed army enabled Abbas to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> in 1598, retake Tabriz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> between 1603 and 1607, expel the Portuguese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> with English naval help in 1622, and seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 1623–1624, a sequence of campaigns that brought the empire to its greatest extent in the 1620s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia.jpg" alt="Abbas I of Persia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Abbas I of Persia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:12_Abu%27l_Hasan_Jahangir_Welcoming_Shah_%27Abbas,_ca._1618,_Freer_Gallery_of_Art,_Washington_DC_(portrait).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bishandas (attribution), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Safavid administration combined Persian bureaucratic traditions inherited from pre-Islamic and medieval Iranian statecraft with the new religious authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shia Islam</a>, creating a structure in which temporal and spiritual power were theoretically united in the Shah but in practice negotiated between the court, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> military aristocracy, the Persian bureaucracy and an increasingly powerful Shia clergy. Civil affairs were directed by the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> (later styled <em>etemad al-dowleh</em>), the religious establishment was supervised by the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadr">sadr</a></em>, and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qurchi-bashi">qurchi-bashi</a></em> commanded the Qizilbash royal guard; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Abbas I</a> the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a></em> system added a further power centre of slave-officials dependent on the Shah, deliberately counterbalancing the Qizilbash. Provincial government was divided between military governors (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beglarbegi">beglarbegi</a></em>) and fiscal administrators (<em>vazir</em>), with strategically sensitive provinces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> held by Qizilbash chiefs in the early period and increasingly by <em>ghulam</em> officials, or converted into crown land, under Abbas I's centralising reforms. The legal system ran on two tracks: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> law, administered by Shia <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a></em> appointed through the <em>sadr</em>, covered personal status, family law and religious matters, while customary royal law (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urf">urf</a></em>) administered through royal courts dealt with criminal, commercial and administrative cases. The conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to Twelver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shiism</a> gave the clerical establishment (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a></em>) a role that expanded significantly from the late seventeenth century, as more activist clerics sought direct influence over appointments, minority communities and public morality, a trend that peaked under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-husayn">Shah Sultan Husayn</a> (r. 1694–1722).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square.jpg" alt="Naqsh-e Jahan Square" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naqsh-i_Jahan_Square,_Jan._2018.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">احمد جمالی مقدم, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Safavid Empire's most consequential contribution to world history was the transformation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> into a predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shia</a> society, a shift accomplished over the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries through compulsion, missionary activity, the recruitment of Arab Shia scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-amil">Jabal Amil</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, and the gradual socialisation of successive generations into Shia practice and identity. This transformation created much of the sectarian geography of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-safavid-wars">Safavid-Ottoman rivalry</a> turned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-shia-divide">Sunni-Shia divide</a> into a geopolitical fault line, and the repeated wars of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries produced frontiers, most durably that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zuhab">Treaty of Zuhab</a> of 1639, whose line broadly anticipates the modern Iran-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> border, reflecting confessional as much as strategic logic. Culturally, the period saw an extraordinary flowering of Persian arts under royal patronage: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-carpet">carpet-weaving</a> reached its classical peak in the court workshops of the sixteenth and early seventeenth centuries, producing masterpieces such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> carpet and the hunting carpets now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a> flourished, first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a>'s celebrated <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a></em> and later in the single-page works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-abbasi">Reza Abbasi</a>; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>'s ensemble of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-mosque">Shah Mosque</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-lotfollah-mosque">Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-qapu">Ali Qapu</a> palace represents one of the supreme achievements of Islamic urban design. Diplomatically, the Safavids pursued engagement with European powers against the common Ottoman threat, exchanging embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from the late sixteenth century and setting precedents for Iran's later dealings with the European state system.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gulnabad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gulnabad.jpg" alt="Battle of Gulnabad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gulnabad">Battle of Gulnabad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battleofgulnabad.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kailanmapper, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Safavid collapse between 1722 and 1736 grew out of structural weaknesses embedded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>'s own reforms, accelerated by a succession of weak rulers and a religious rigidity that alienated much of the empire's diverse population. Abbas I's answer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> problem, a <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a></em> establishment loyal personally to the Shah, worked during his lifetime but concentrated everything in the person of the ruler, while his suspicion of his own sons, one executed and others blinded, left no capable adult heir; future princes were thereafter raised in the seclusion of the harem. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia">Shah Safi</a> (r. 1629–1642) began his reign by purging experienced generals, officials and royal relatives, and his successors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-persia">Abbas II</a> (r. 1642–1666) being a partial exception, proved progressively less capable of meeting either military or political challenges. Decline accelerated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-husayn">Shah Sultan Husayn</a> (r. 1694–1722), early in whose reign the influential cleric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-baqir-majlesi">Muhammad Baqir Majlesi</a> (d. 1699) promoted pressure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> and Christians that alienated minority communities. In 1709 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzai Afghans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, Sunnis resentful of Safavid rule, rose under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mir Wais Hotak</a>. In 1722 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mir Mahmud Hotak</a>, with perhaps 20,000 men, defeated a much larger Safavid army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gulnabad">Battle of Gulnabad</a> in March and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-isfahan">Siege of Isfahan</a>, which lasted some seven months; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Husayn surrendered in October 1722 and personally placed the crown on Mahmud's head. The able commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Quli Afshar</a> later expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and ruled through puppet Safavid shahs before convening an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughan-assembly">assembly on the Mughan plain</a> in 1736 and having himself proclaimed Shah, formally ending the Safavid dynasty after 235 years.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Safavid Empire's most enduring legacy is the Shia Islamic identity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, a transformation so thorough and so institutionally embedded that it outlived the dynasty by three centuries and continues to shape Iranian culture, statecraft and geopolitical orientation.</em></p>

